## `DeviceContext`

```
from max.gpu.host import DeviceContext

var ctx = DeviceContext()
```

Every allocation, copy and launch goes through it. {#0F766E}It is the handle to one device, not a global mode you switch on.{/}

## How many blocks, and how many threads per block

A grid is made of thread blocks, organized over one, two or three dimensions; each block is divided into threads.

{#0F766E}Together they decide how many threads run — and therefore how much bounds checking your kernel needs.{/}

## An ordinary `def` that you launch instead of calling

It is a normal Mojo function; what makes it a kernel is being passed to `enqueue_function` as a compile-time parameter.

{#0F766E}No decorator marks it. The launch site is what distinguishes it.{/}

## The accelerator host and runtime APIs, into `max`

{#C62828}`DeviceContext` and friends are no longer in `std`.{/} They are in `max.gpu.host`.

{#0F766E}Rule of thumb: code that runs *on* the device is `std.gpu`; code that *drives* the device is `max.gpu`.{/}

## The call returns before the work happens

Every GPU operation is asynchronous — launches, allocations and copies are queued on the device, not performed inline.

{#0F766E}The prefix is on the method name so you cannot forget.{/}

## Fixed-width values and buffer views

Sizes and scalars are passed by value; data is passed as a tensor view over device memory.

{#C62828}A host pointer passed to a kernel is not valid on the device.{/}

## NVIDIA, AMD and Apple Metal

Selected at build time with `--target-accelerator`, alongside the CPU architecture and feature set.

{#0F766E}Same source; the target decides which code is generated.{/}

## `ctx.enqueue_create_host_buffer[dtype](size)`

It produces a `HostBuffer` — CPU-accessible memory that the device can also reach for transfers.

{#0F766E}Allocation is enqueued like everything else.{/}

## `ctx.enqueue_create_buffer[dtype](size)`

It produces a `DeviceBuffer` in the device's global memory.

{#0F766E}The unqualified name is the device one — the host case is the one that has to say so.{/}

## Which processor can dereference it

`HostBuffer` is CPU-accessible; `DeviceBuffer` lives in GPU global memory and is not addressable from the host.

{#C62828}They are distinct types precisely so you cannot confuse them.{/}

## `ctx.enqueue_copy(dst_buf=..., src_buf=...)`

The same call serves both directions — the buffer types say which way the data is going.

{#0F766E}It is enqueued, so it has not happened when the call returns.{/}

## So global and shared memory cannot be mixed up

`AddressSpace` distinguishes host memory, GPU global memory and shared memory, and `Span` carries the parameter too.

{#0F766E}A shared-memory pointer will not silently pass where a global one is expected. The type system does the checking the hardware will not.{/}

## The buffer, with Mojo's ordinary ownership rules

A `DeviceBuffer` frees its allocation when it is destroyed, and it moves rather than copies.

{#0F766E}GPU allocations get the same lifetime discipline as everything else — no manual free, no double free.{/}

## Until the work has actually completed

{#C62828}Enqueueing is not executing.{/} A buffer destroyed after the launch but before synchronization can be freed while the kernel is still reading it.

{#0F766E}The one place where the asynchrony can defeat ownership reasoning.{/}

## The bus is far slower than device memory

A kernel can read global memory at a rate the host link cannot approach.

{#0F766E}Which is why the usual shape is: copy once, run many kernels, copy back once — not copy per step.{/}

## Registers, then shared memory, then global

Registers are per-thread; shared memory is per-block and on-chip; global memory is device-wide and off-chip.

{#0F766E}Tiling exists to move a working set up this hierarchy once and reuse it many times.{/}

## A mapping from logical coordinates to a linear index

It is what turns `(row, col)` into an offset — and it can also map GPU threads onto the tiles of data they own.

{#0F766E}Separating the layout from the storage is what lets one kernel work over several memory arrangements.{/}

## Shape is how many; stride is how far apart

Shape gives the extent of each dimension. Stride gives the step in the linear index when that dimension advances by one.

{#0F766E}Two tensors can share a shape and have completely different strides — that is the whole point of the abstraction.{/}

## It decides whether neighbouring threads read neighbouring addresses

Consecutive threads reading consecutive addresses is one wide memory transaction; the same threads reading down a column is many.

{#C62828}Same arithmetic, order-of-magnitude difference in bandwidth.{/}

## Giving each block a rectangular piece of the data

The layout maps a block's threads onto one tile, so the block can stage that tile in shared memory and reuse it.

{#0F766E}The layout expresses the decomposition, so the kernel body does not have to compute it.{/}

## A tensor with its memory layout stated explicitly, via a `Layout`

It is the general form: the layout is a value the tensor carries.

{#0F766E}Use it when the arrangement is what you are reasoning about.{/}

## `TileTensor` carries compile-time layout information

Knowing the layout at compile time lets index arithmetic fold away and access vectorize.

{#0F766E}`TileTensor` is what the tutorials hand to kernels; `LayoutTensor` is the more general type underneath.{/}

## Layouts whose dimensions are not known until run time

The compile-time path cannot express a shape read from a file or a request.

{#0F766E}The trade is explicit: dynamic dimensions cost you the folding that a compile-time layout gets.{/}

## The hierarchical integer tuple layouts are built from

Shapes and strides are `IntTuple`s, and being hierarchical is what lets a layout describe nested tiling rather than a flat grid.

{#0F766E}A shape can itself contain a shape.{/}

## Copying data between `TileTensor`s

Moving a tile from global into shared memory is the operation, and it is common enough to have its own utilities.

{#0F766E}Layout-aware, so the copy knows the arrangement on both sides.{/}

## Access to the hardware matrix units

High-performance matrix operations, including asynchronous variants, mapped onto Tensor Core instructions.

{#0F766E}The layout requirements are strict — this is where the tiling has to match what the hardware expects, exactly.{/}

## Asynchronous bulk data movement

The transfer is issued and proceeds while the block computes on data it already has.

{#0F766E}Overlapping movement with compute is what keeps the arithmetic units busy instead of waiting on memory.{/}

## The layout tells the compiler which accesses are contiguous

Contiguous elements can be loaded as one wide vector rather than one at a time.

{#0F766E}You get it by describing the layout accurately, not by writing vector code.{/}

## Every thread in the block must reach it

{#C62828}A `barrier()` inside a conditional is a common source of deadlock.{/} Threads that skip the branch never arrive, and the ones that did wait forever.

{#0F766E}Put the barrier outside the `if`, not inside it.{/}

## No — it is redundant

Warp shuffles and reductions provide implicit synchronization.

{#0F766E}An extra `syncwarp()` there is a cost with no effect, and it suggests the author was unsure of the semantics.{/}

## `shuffle_up`, `shuffle_down`, `shuffle_xor`, `shuffle_idx`, `broadcast`

From `gpu.primitives.warp`. Each lane receives a value from another lane: at a fixed offset down or up, at an XOR of its lane id, at an arbitrary lane, or from lane 0.

{#0F766E}Register-to-register — no memory involved.{/}

## Every thread in the warp must reach the shuffle

{#C62828}With a full mask, all threads must arrive — even those that will not use the result.{/}

{#0F766E}Same trap as `barrier()`: a collective operation hidden inside a branch that not every thread takes.{/}

## `max`, `min`, `sum`, and `prefix_sum`

From `gpu.primitives.warp`. `prefix_sum[exclusive=False]` gives an inclusive scan; set the parameter for an exclusive one.

{#0F766E}A warp reduction is far cheaper than the shared-memory equivalent.{/}

## The same set, one level up

`gpu.primitives.block` provides `sum`, `max`, `min`, `prefix_sum`, and `broadcast(val, src_thread=0)`.

{#0F766E}Reduce within each warp first, then across warps — which is what the block primitives already do for you.{/}

## Stepping does not work reliably on the GPU

{#C62828}Step Over, Step Into and Step Out are all unreliable there.{/}

Set a breakpoint with `break filename.mojo:line_number` and continue. {#0F766E}`info registers` inspects state; the `cuda` command switches focus between threads and blocks.{/}

## The wrong answer is often stable

A missing `barrier()` produces the right result whenever the scheduler happens to cooperate, which on one machine can be every time.

{#0F766E}Reason about which thread wrote a value and which barrier makes that write visible. Testing will not find it for you.{/}
